WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:03.200
So we already know how to take the absolute value of a number.

00:03.200 --> 00:10.280
For instance, the absolute value of negative two we know is equal to positive two.

00:10.280 --> 00:16.550
And similarly, we know that the absolute value of positive two is also equal to two.

00:16.550 --> 00:21.410
And remember that the reason we know that is because the absolute value is really asking us for the

00:21.410 --> 00:23.440
distance from zero.

00:23.450 --> 00:31.610
So if we sketch here a simple number line and we position zero at the center, well then we know that

00:31.610 --> 00:36.850
negative two might be right about here and positive two is here.

00:36.860 --> 00:42.110
And when we're saying let's take the absolute value of negative two, what we're asking for there is

00:42.110 --> 00:44.810
the distance from negative two.

00:45.550 --> 00:46.150
To zero.

00:46.150 --> 00:48.460
And we know that that is two units.

00:48.490 --> 00:51.970
Well, in the same way, the distance from 0 to 2.

00:53.230 --> 01:01.360
Is two units, so both negative two and positive two have a distance of two units from zero, which

01:01.360 --> 01:07.210
is why the absolute value of both negative two and positive two turns out to be positive two.

01:07.240 --> 01:11.290
It's two units or a distance of two units from zero.

01:11.320 --> 01:18.010
So we already know how to address absolute value when we have a single number like this.

01:18.010 --> 01:21.620
But what about when we have an expression like this one?

01:21.640 --> 01:26.380
This is the absolute value of the expression negative three minus four.

01:26.410 --> 01:33.100
Well, when we change from just a single number to an expression, the only thing that changes is that

01:33.100 --> 01:36.460
we have to evaluate the expression first.

01:36.460 --> 01:42.110
And then only once we've finished evaluating the expression do we apply the absolute value.

01:42.130 --> 01:43.690
It's never the other order.

01:43.690 --> 01:48.530
We never start by taking the absolute value and then simplifying the inside.

01:48.550 --> 01:52.600
We always start by simplifying the inside first and then second.

01:52.630 --> 01:54.960
We apply the absolute value at the end.

01:54.980 --> 02:00.710
In other words, with an expression like this one, we want to think about just what's on the inside

02:00.710 --> 02:01.070
here.

02:01.070 --> 02:07.130
So just the negative three minus four, Let's in other words, completely ignore that we have an absolute

02:07.130 --> 02:10.070
value at all and just look at negative three minus four.

02:10.070 --> 02:13.190
So we know that negative three minus four is negative seven.

02:13.830 --> 02:21.480
Which means this whole expression simplifies to negative seven and therefore that we can rewrite this

02:21.510 --> 02:26.130
absolute value here as the absolute value of negative seven.

02:26.370 --> 02:33.450
And we already know that that is equal to positive seven, which means this expression here is equivalent

02:33.450 --> 02:36.330
to or simplifies to positive seven.

02:36.330 --> 02:41.380
And just realize that we simplify first and then apply the absolute value at the end.

02:41.400 --> 02:43.180
So let's do one more example.

02:43.200 --> 02:47.250
Again, we'll simplify first, then apply the absolute value at the end.

02:47.280 --> 02:55.500
So here we have two plus the absolute value of five minus nine, minus the absolute value of negative

02:55.500 --> 02:56.170
three.

02:56.190 --> 03:00.930
So we can simplify that five minus nine, five minus nine is negative four.

03:01.140 --> 03:03.660
So let's rewrite this as two plus.

03:03.690 --> 03:07.980
We'll leave the absolute value and we'll just simplify what's inside it.

03:07.980 --> 03:10.140
So five minus nine becomes negative four.

03:10.290 --> 03:13.280
Negative three is already simplified as much as it can be.

03:13.290 --> 03:15.760
We can't simplify just a single number.

03:15.760 --> 03:18.430
So let's also go ahead and leave that.

03:18.460 --> 03:24.970
Now we have all of the expressions inside the absolute values simplified, which means now we can go

03:24.970 --> 03:28.970
ahead and actually apply the absolute value to the number inside.

03:28.990 --> 03:35.860
So we can say two plus the absolute value of negative four is four and the absolute value of negative

03:35.860 --> 03:36.700
three is three.

03:36.700 --> 03:43.030
So we keep the subtraction here or the minus and we say absolute value of negative three is three.

03:43.180 --> 03:51.130
And so now we have two plus four is six and six minus three is three, which means that this original

03:51.130 --> 03:56.290
expression here, two plus the absolute value of five minus nine, minus the absolute value of negative

03:56.290 --> 03:59.710
three, that is equivalent to just three.

03:59.710 --> 04:03.580
Or we can simplify that expression to just positive three.

04:03.610 --> 04:09.010
So again, the takeaway here is whenever we're taking the absolute value of some expression instead

04:09.010 --> 04:15.250
of just a single number, we always remember to first simplify the expression as much as possible,

04:15.250 --> 04:20.470
then at the end apply the absolute value, not the other way around.
